<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 40 "Feel Flow" M2 — the LANE HEATMAP, the pixel gate.

  A track-level groove analysis (M1/M1b: STrack::startFeelFlowBounce()) shows
  up on the arranger as a bottom band on the track's OWN lane, tinted per
  column from the "groove.res" compliance scalar. This is the AC 4 case:
  false (never analyzed) then analyze then true (with a minPixels floor and
  the colour relations, plus a PNG grab) then a staling edit then false again.

  THE SLOT CORRECTION (kickoff AC 2). The design's v2 text said "right after
  drawChildSumOverlay", i.e. BEHIND the clip loop, which paints the band
  invisible on exactly the clip-covered lanes this feature targets (a clip
  fills its rect with QColor(160,160,160) and would paint straight over
  anything drawn earlier). The band is instead drawn LAST, after every clip,
  as a ~20%-of-lane-height strip along the bottom
  (STrackRendererInline::drawFeelFlowBand). This case's single track carries
  ONE clip spanning nearly the whole visible width, so it is the one
  configuration that would have caught the wrong slot outright: an
  expectOverlay="true" assertion over a lane covered edge-to-edge by a clip
  body PASSES on the corrected code and would have measured 0 overlay pixels
  on the v2 slot.

  THE COLOUR RELATION (assert-lane-overlay's own gate, proposal 39's
  discipline, D4): every band pixel must be strictly LIGHTER than the lane
  fill and strictly DARKER than the clip body grey. sFeelFlowTint()
  (strackrndrinline.cpp) gets this BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by picking
  numbers: it linearly interpolates every channel between the lane's own
  fill colour and the fixed clip-body grey, and Rec.601 luminance is a
  LINEAR function of the channels, so a mixing fraction strictly inside
  (0,1) always lands strictly between the two luminances, whatever this
  track's fill colour happens to be, and whichever of the two backgrounds
  (bare lane fill beyond the clip, or the clip body itself) a given column's
  alpha-blended pixel actually composites over. Compliance modulates BOTH
  the mixing fraction and the alpha (low compliance = bolder, closer to the
  clip grey, reads "hotter"; high compliance = fainter, closer to the fill),
  and the two never fight.

  THE HOP DOMAIN (AC 2's "placement arithmetic only, no warp map"). The
  bounce is a whole-project render starting at project frame 0
  (sfeelflowbounce.cpp), so a "groove.res" hop's frame position IS a
  track-timeline frame, and STrackRendererInline::drawFeelFlowBand divides
  ctx.getTimeOf(x) by hopFrames directly, no clip-relative offset, no
  sourceToWarpedExact, anywhere in the paint path. The fixture
  (../groove/a_offset15.wav, the same one feel_flow_bounce_stale.qxa uses)
  is ~778452 frames (~16.2 s @ 48 kHz); the default arranger zoom is
  30 px/second (SStdMixerView::secondWidth_), so a 900 px grab shows a 30 s
  window and the whole clip, plus material AFTER the clip's own extent,
  which is exactly where hop >= nHops and the band correctly stops.

  THE STALING EDIT is set-track-volume, deliberately NOT the insert-plugin
  feel_flow_bounce_stale.qxa uses: a second, independent path to the same
  content-epoch bump (STrack::onTrackVolumeChanged -> invalidateRenderPath(),
  which is what feeds twGainStage, downstream of which sits the root
  component the bounce reads and STrack::feelFlowStale() checks).

  WHY THE "FALSE" ASSERTIONS USE maxPixels, NOT A LITERAL ZERO. Measured on
  this exact fixture: a freshly-added, never-analyzed clip already
  contributes ~1100-1200 pixels in the "strictly between fill and clip body"
  luminance range, EVEN with bandOnly="true" scoping the scan to the bottom
  fifth of the lane (a waveform's troughs reach the bottom of the clip rect
  as often as the top), the clip's own waveform paint, found empirically
  while writing this case, not a defect in the feel-flow band. It is
  UNCHANGED by staling: 1131 pixels both before the first analysis and after
  the staling edit, byte-identical in the log. So the negative assertions
  name that noise floor explicitly (with margin) via `maxPixels`
  (sassertenvelopeaction.h's own doc has the full measurement) rather than
  asserting a zero the classifier cannot honestly reach over real waveform
  content, the same hazard folder_sum_preview.qxa's "NOT gated" note
  already named for a CLIP as a negative control, worked around there by
  using only clip-free lanes, which this feature's own subject cannot be.
  The gap between the noise floor (~1131) and the fresh band (~8779, 8x
  higher) is what actually carries the "nothing paints when stale or absent"
  claim; assert-groove-aspect's `stale=` attribute gates the exact boolean
  (STrack::feelFlowStale()) the paint code branches on.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="feel_flow_heatmap">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- The grid crosses every lane and would land in the pixel histogram as
         chrome; the overlay gate wants a clean band (proposal 39's own
         convention for assert-lane-overlay). -->
    <grid-disable/>
    <sidecar-root/>

    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../groove/a_offset15.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- ============ AC 4 (1/3): never analyzed -> NOTHING paints ========= -->
    <!-- STrack::feelFlowStale() is true (no bounce ever completed, the safe
         answer per sfeelflowbounce.h's own doc) whether or not a holder even
         exists yet, so the band contributes NOTHING beyond the clip's own
         intrinsic waveform noise (measured ~1131 px, see the header note);
         maxPixels names that floor with margin. -->
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="0" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="300"
                         expectOverlay="false" bandOnly="true" maxPixels="2500"/>
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="groove.res" trackPath="0" stale="1"
                          expectExists="false"/>

    <feel-flow-analyze target="track" trackPath="0"/>
    <wait-analysis timeoutMs="60000"/>

    <!-- Sanity: the analysis actually produced data before asking the paint
         path to show it (fresh, in-bounds record count, feel_flow_bounce_
         stale.qxa's own bracket). -->
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="groove.res" trackPath="0" stale="0"
                          expectExists="true" minRecords="1500" maxRecords="1650"/>

    <!-- ========= AC 4 (2/3): fresh -> the band paints, in-relation ======= -->
    <!-- Only wait-analysis stands between the analyze and this assertion
         (AC 3: repaint rides captureRevalidated() with no manual poke, the
         same notifyCaptureRevalidated() the analysis job's completion
         already queues for the badge). minPixels=2500 sits ABOVE the
         measured noise floor's ceiling, so this genuinely proves the band
         itself, not the clip's own waveform. -->
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="0" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="300"
                         minPixels="2500" bandOnly="true"
                         grabPng="feel_flow_heatmap.png"/>

    <!-- =========== AC 4 (3/3): a staling edit -> NOTHING again =========== -->
    <set-track-volume trackPath="0" volume="-6"/>
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="groove.res" trackPath="0" stale="1"
                          expectExists="true"/>
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="0" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="300"
                         expectOverlay="false" bandOnly="true" maxPixels="2500"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
